{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Aaron drew near to the altar.</b> It alludes to the words of our Sages of blessed memory, who said (Yerushalmi Makkot 2:6): they asked of prophecy, ״The sinner — what is his punishment?״ It answered, ״The soul that sins, it shall die״; but from the side of HaShem's mercy He said, ״Let him bring a sacrifice״ — for the essence of the act of the offering is the intention of the one who brings it, since he is the one liable for every act that is done to that offering, to slaughter it and to burn it on the fires, and this intention serves him for atonement. And this is the verse's saying <b>and Aaron drew near to the altar</b> — meaning, he himself prepared to bear the guilt of his soul, and drew near to the altar, meaning, to slaughter it for the fires, and performed the slaughter in its place for the calf, from the side of HaShem's mercy that He was appeased to accept its substitute." "<b>And I saw</b> to give attention to the matter in light of what has come before us (Tanchuma, Shofetim) — that HaShem swore to sustain the world and its creatures through justice, and whoever says the Holy One, blessed be He, is lenient — may his intestines be loosened (Yerushalmi Shekalim 5:1). If so, if the soul that sins — its judgment is that it shall die, as the prophet says (Ezekiel 18:4) — how can he bring a sacrifice and be exempt? And it appears that there is a sound reason in the matter according to justice, for when a person sins, his spiritual condition is no longer in the condition of a human, on the path of their saying of blessed memory (Sotah 3a): a person does not transgress unless a spirit of folly enters him, and he becomes a beast; and on the day he repents, the spirit returns to its humanity and rises from the level of beast to the level of human. Is it fitting to put to death this human in place of the beast? Therefore he brings a likeness of the sinner — which is a beast — and joins with it in the distress of the thought that it is fitting to execute this judgment upon it. Accordingly, the portion of the sensing spirit saves the person through this association, and this is what the verse says (Psalms 36:7): ״Man and beast You save, HaShem״ — for the bestial part within man, when it is joined with the human — which is the aspect of the intellect — is saved through it, meaning, at its side, so that it not be punished when it is not worthy of punishment. And in this, even according to strict justice, the offering of a sacrifice for sin is on the line of justice, as it says (ibid.): ״Your judgments are the great deep; man and beast You save, HaShem.״"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:43.815065615Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}